  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows an occluded low centered \nover southeast Ontario with a cold front stretched southward \nthrough the eastern Great Lakes Region and arching back westward\nthrough the Ohio Valley. Further northwest, an expansive area \nof high pressure is noted over the Plains states/Upper \nMississippi Valley. This will expand east-southeast into the \nMidwest states today, keeping things comfortable and dry the \nnext several days. High temperatures through Saturday will be \nseasonable in the low to middle 80s, with cool overnight lows in\nthe 60s.\n\nStrong upper ridging noted over the western US late this week will \nshift east this weekend, raising mid-level heights to around 590-594 \ndm by Sunday. Southerly return flow at the surface will bring a big \npush of a warmer and more humid air northward. High temperatures \nlook to peak in the upper 80s to low 90s beginning Sunday with heat \nindices in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees as early as Monday. \nThe Extreme Forecast Index has high values (0.8-0.9) paired \nwith a Shift of Tails of 1, implying there is relatively high \nconfidence in an anomalous late summer heat event occuring. \nAlthough high temperature records will be out of reach, record \nwarm lows in the 70s could be tied or broken during this \nstretch.\n\nSimultaneously, a northern stream shortwave trough will lift from \nthe northwest US into the southern Canadian Prairies early next\nweek, sending a cold front sagging southward from the Plains \nstates into parts of the Midwest. Although a vast majority of \nthe precipitation associated with the front looks to remain \nnorth of here, it will be highly dependent on the depth and \nstrength of the trough. As of now, there is at least a low \nchance for showers and storms clipping northern parts of the \nforecast area Monday through Wednesday. Further south, mostly \ndry conditions are favored over the next 7 days.",
